      "title": "What is Binary Search Tree?",
      "lectureOrder": 158,
      "videoOrder": 157,
      "sourceHash": "c427a89141ebb0fee8597603dd65e8082e9d9d16d4f7b3325d9a826f1c0b8444",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 5.24,
          "end": 5.69,
          "en": "All right.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 5.69,
          "end": 9.98,
          "en": "In this section we are going to look at another type of tree which is binary search tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 10.01,
          "end": 14.6,
          "en": "Basically binary search tree is another type of binary tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 14.6,
          "end": 17.9,
          "en": "So it's a binary tree with additional properties.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 17.93,
          "end": 20.18,
          "en": "Now the first property is in the left subtree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 20.18,
          "end": 24.41,
          "en": "The value of node is less than or equal to its parents node value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 24.41,
          "end": 30.2,
          "en": "And the second property is that in the right subtree, the value of node is greater than its parent",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 30.23,
          "end": 31.07,
          "en": "nodes value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 31.1,
          "end": 32.66,
          "en": "Now let's look at what does it mean.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 32.66,
          "end": 35.09,
          "en": "So let's say we have a binary tree like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 35.09,
          "end": 37.04,
          "en": "So in this case this is a binary search tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 37.04,
          "end": 39.89,
          "en": "So how do we know that we can know it by comparing the values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 39.89,
          "end": 42.44,
          "en": "So here the root node is 70.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 42.47,
          "end": 48.44,
          "en": "Now according to the binary search tree property the nodes in the left subtree has to be less or equal",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 48.44,
          "end": 49.04,
          "en": "to the root node.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 49.04,
          "end": 53,
          "en": "So as you can see here we have 5030, 6020 and 40.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 53,
          "end": 55.73,
          "en": "And all these nodes are smaller than the root node.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 55.73,
          "end": 59.96,
          "en": "And when it comes to the second property on the right we have the nodes which is greater than the root",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 59.96,
          "end": 60.17,
          "en": "node.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 60.17,
          "end": 63.26,
          "en": "So we see that on the right we have 9080 and 100.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 63.26,
          "end": 65.22,
          "en": "And all of them are greater than 70.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 65.25,
          "end": 68.78999999999999,
          "en": "So these rules are applicable for each subtrees over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 68.78999999999999,
          "end": 72.69,
          "en": "So if you look at, for example the left subtree, we can see that in the left subtree the root node",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 72.72,
          "end": 73.71000000000001,
          "en": "is 50.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 73.71000000000001,
          "end": 76.8,
          "en": "And in its left we have 30 which is less than 50.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 76.8,
          "end": 80.03999999999999,
          "en": "And on the right we have 60 which is greater than 50.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 80.07,
          "end": 81.99,
          "en": "It's applicable for this left subtree also.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 81.99,
          "end": 87,
          "en": "So 30 is greater than left subtree which is 20 and less than 40 which is right subtree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 87.03,
          "end": 90.12,
          "en": "So here again these rules are applicable for 90.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 90.15,
          "end": 93.27000000000001,
          "en": "80 is less than 90 and 100 is greater than 90.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 93.27000000000001,
          "end": 97.02000000000001,
          "en": "So as you can see for each subtree these rules have to be valid.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 97.02000000000001,
          "end": 99.57,
          "en": "So in this case this is called binary search tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 99.6,
          "end": 102.39,
          "en": "Now the question is why do we need binary search tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 102.42,
          "end": 106.08,
          "en": "A binary search tree does not store an index of its elements.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 106.08,
          "end": 112.95,
          "en": "Instead, it relies on its implicit structure left and right of each node to keep a record of where",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 112.95,
          "end": 113.94,
          "en": "each element is.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 113.94,
          "end": 118.97999999999999,
          "en": "The result is we can do the insertion and deletion faster than the other data structures.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 118.97999999999999,
          "end": 124.8,
          "en": "This is because it is structure makes the insertion and deletion of nodes very fast.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 124.8,
          "end": 130.35,
          "en": "So instead of traversing every element sequentially until the right one is found, which is how we work",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 130.35,
          "end": 131.19,
          "en": "in case of arrays.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 131.22,
          "end": 135.78,
          "en": "Here we can only traverse half the tree and half the tree and half the tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 135.78,
          "end": 139.77,
          "en": "So let's say we want to insert the node after this 20 over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 139.77,
          "end": 142.23,
          "en": "So in this case we will look at the left subtree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 142.23,
          "end": 145.35,
          "en": "Then we are going to look at one more time the left subtree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 145.35,
          "end": 147.63,
          "en": "And we are going to look at the left node.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 147.63,
          "end": 153.42000000000002,
          "en": "So as you can see the process is halving the tree in each step instead of traversing all nodes.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 153.42000000000002,
          "end": 159.32999999999998,
          "en": "That's why the result is faster, because every time we are making the tree half and half.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 159.32999999999998,
          "end": 162.03,
          "en": "So that is why we need binary search tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 162.06,
          "end": 166.41,
          "en": "Now let's go ahead and see how binary search tree constructor looks like.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
